                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <p rendition="#center">[Translated abstract from letter of September 13, received by
                    Hon. John R. Planten, consul-general of the Netherlands at New York, from the
                    governor of Curaçao.]</p>
                <p>By decree of August 28, 1888, the import duties on salt for the entire colony of
                    Curaçao (comprising Curaçao, Oruba, Buen Ayre, Little Curaçao, Little Buen Ayre,
                    St. Martin, St. Eustatius, and Saba) were abolished and they have never been
                    reimposed.</p>
                <p>It is probable that the Washington authorities were misled by consulting the
                    tariff laws of 1892, in which, as is the case every year, also the article salt
                    is mentioned, and they must have overlooked the fact that these published tariff
                    laws refer as well to export as to import duties; and, for instance, as St.
                    Martin and Buen Ayre pay export duties, the mention of the duties as regards
                    salt simply refers to these export duties and not to import duties, which latter
                    were abolished since 18—, and never reimposed.</p>
            </div>
